$DIR/main.p:10:46: cannot return f(), which points into the closure inner() returns, built in the function literal's frame
    f() points into the closure inner() returns, built in the function literal's
    frame. inner() returns a closure, which it builds, with the variables it
    uses, in a region of its caller's frame, the function literal's (at
    main.p:10); they live as long as that frame (section 11.5). That frame is
    reclaimed when the function literal returns, so f() would point at memory
    that no longer holds the value.
    Go would move this memory to the heap, and its garbage collector would free
    it later. Plutonium has no garbage collector and never moves memory to the
    heap behind the program's back, so that every allocation is visible in the
    code.
    Return the value itself, which copies it, or allocate it so that it outlives
    the function.
    (see "Views" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.3)
    (run "pluto explain E3001" for a longer explanation)
